      "page_title": "Pin, Future Size & What .await Really Is",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Why Pin Exists"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "A local borrowed across an await points into the same struct",
            "Futures are self-referential — moving them dangles pointers",
            "`Pin<&mut T>` promises no move after the first poll",
            "`Unpin` = safe to move anyway; most hand-written futures are",
            "Compiler-generated futures are NOT `Unpin`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "rust",
          "lines": [
            "let fut = fetch(url);",
            "tokio::pin!(fut);      // pinned on the stack",
            "std::pin::pin!(other); // std flavour",
            "let b = Box::pin(fetch(url)); // heap"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Future Size"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Fact",
            "Detail"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Size rule",
              "max of live state, not sum"
            ],
            [
              "Symptom",
              "stack blowup, huge moves"
            ],
            [
              "Fix",
              "`Box::pin` it"
            ],
            [
              "Also",
              "shrink locals held across awaits"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Recursion Needs Indirection",
          "items": [
            "An `async fn` calling itself has infinite size",
            "Wrap the recursive call in `Box::pin`",
            "Or use the `async_recursion` crate"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "What .await Really Is"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Not a syscall, not a thread yield",
            "It is a `return Poll::Pending` from the state machine",
            "No allocation per await — the chain is one flat struct",
            "That flatness is the key win over GC languages"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "Start",
              "poll",
              "Pending",
              "wake",
              "resume",
              "Ready"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Read The Shape",
          "text": "Name the locals crossing each await and you can predict the future's size."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Suspend Only Here",
          "ink": "accent"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "An await point is the only place an async fn can suspend",
            "Between them, execution is uninterrupted",
            "`.await` on a ready future returns instantly — no yield",
            "Cooperative: the runtime cannot preempt you, ever",
            "State needed after an await must live in the future"
          ]
        }
      ]
